## 1.5 Self-Healing Flow

The self-healing system is implemented in [workflow.py → fix_failures()](file:///b:/Project/QA_AntiG/qa-agent/backend/agent/workflow.py#L366-L474):

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[execute_tests] --> B{Has failures?}
    B -->|No| C[generate_report]
    B -->|Yes| D{retry_count >= 1?}
    D -->|Yes| C
    D -->|No| E[fix_failures]
    E --> F[For each failed test]
    F --> G[Get original test case]
    G --> H[Build base prompt with context]
    H --> I[Append error + logs to repair prompt]
    I --> J[LLM generates new script]
    J --> K[Strip markdown fences]
    K --> L[Update DB: delete old + insert new]
    L --> M[Update state scripts list]
    M --> N[Clear execution_results]
    N --> O[Increment retry_count → 1]
    O --> P[should_retry → "retry"]
    P --> A
```

> [!WARNING]
> **Current limitation**: Self-healing is capped at 1 retry. The repair prompt includes the original base prompt (Selenium-specific) plus the error context. This means the healing is also Selenium-coupled.

---

## 1.6 Browser Execution Flow

**Current model**: Out-of-process execution via subprocess.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[FastAPI Process] -->|write tmp .py file| B[Temp Script File]
    B -->|subprocess.run| C[New Python Process]
    C -->|import selenium| D[ChromeDriver Manager]
    D -->|download/cache| E[ChromeDriver Binary]
    E -->|launch| F[Chrome Browser]
    F -->|execute test steps| G[DOM Interactions]
    G -->|stdout: SCREENSHOT_PATH, BROWSER, etc.| C
    C -->|exit code + stdout/stderr| A
    A -->|parse metadata from stdout| H[ExecutionResult]
```

**Key observations**:
- Each test spawns a **new Python process** and a **new browser instance**
- No browser pooling or reuse
- ChromeDriver is downloaded/cached by `webdriver-manager` on first run
- Metadata is communicated via **stdout parsing** (SCREENSHOT_PATH:, BROWSER:, VIDEO_PATH:, etc.)
- 60-second hard timeout via `subprocess.run(timeout=60)`
- Script runs synchronously within subprocess; async boundary is `asyncio.to_thread()`

---

## 1.7 WebSocket Streaming Lifecycle

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant FE as Frontend (Socket.IO Client)
    participant SIO as Socket.IO Server (/ws)
    participant API as FastAPI Routes
    participant EX as Execution Service

    FE->>SIO: connect
    SIO-->>FE: connected

    FE->>SIO: subscribe_execution({execution_id})
    SIO->>SIO: enter_room(sid, execution_id)
    SIO-->>FE: subscribed

    API->>EX: run_test_background()
    EX->>SIO: emit_started(execution_id)
    SIO-->>FE: execution_started

    EX->>SIO: emit_progress(execution_id, step, total, msg)
    SIO-->>FE: execution_progress

    EX->>SIO: emit_completed(execution_id, status, duration)
    SIO-->>FE: execution_completed

    FE->>SIO: unsubscribe_execution({execution_id})
    SIO->>SIO: leave_room(sid, execution_id)
```

> [!NOTE]
> WebSocket is **engine-agnostic** — it emits events about execution status, not browser internals. This is a clean boundary that requires **zero changes** during migration.

